    {
      "id": "51bc489bf762",
      "kind": "character",
      "chamber": 2,
      "seed": 2123084958,
      "data": {
        "lines": [
          "Up top I chased things that go to the light. Down here I had to unlearn it. Down here the light is the thing.",
          "Auntie Coral said don't blink at them. I blinked. Now one of them follows me, blinking, half a second late, like it's learning.",
          "Lumen wanted to come. I told him his lamp would be a lie this deep. He lit it anyway, somewhere above us. I can almost hear it.",
          "The lighthouse fell in 1911. Nobody told it there were no ships. It kept the pattern. Short, short, long. So did something else.",
          "If you signal, mean it. Something out there is counting, and it does not forget a rhythm."
        ],
        "look": {
          "accent": "#d8ff5e",
          "coat": "#1b1a2e",
          "hat": "net"
        },
        "name": "The moth collector"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014353104
    },
    {
      "id": "f6b483c69e6c",
      "kind": "note",
      "chamber": 2,
      "seed": 1248438885,
      "data": {
        "text": "You built the trench and I thought that was the loneliest a place could be made. Then you gave it a reply. Now I stand on the silt flashing my little lamp into the black and the black flashes back, late, in a colour that is almost mine, and I cannot tell whether it is kindness or hunger. I have realised, standing here with the snow coming down through the water, that I would take either. Please do not tell me which it is. (Page 4 of 6.)",
        "title": "Letters to the Architect, page 4"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014353104
    },
